# Corpus unit bam/C06 — gap (unmet mandatory single-variable CQ; batch-size / lambda_t confound)
#
# Source: m0/annotations/bam.yaml C06 (causal, dose_response; corpus pin 62e9b54). Headline: BaM
# converges faster at larger batch sizes (dose levels up to B ~= D) measured in gradient
# evaluations, while ADVI and GSM show marginal or no improvement from larger batches (E01-E04,
# Fig 5.1).
#
# Lowering notes (conventions: corpus-units/LOWERING.md):
#   * dose_response lowers through the component_ablation family (scheme family map); conclusion
#     contributes(batch_size, bam, convergence_speed). This is a three-way contrast; the binding
#     conjunct is the positive BaM effect, and the ADVI/GSM null halves stay in `nl` and refs.
#   * protocol_parity is annotated met (improvement is measured in gradient evaluations, not
#     iterations; E04 fixes a 3000-gradient-evaluation budget).
#   * single_variable is annotated UNMET-GAP: lambda_t = BD couples regularization strength to
#     batch size, so increasing B simultaneously increases lambda_t; only E04's per-batch-size
#     pilot breaks this confound. The batch-size effect is not isolated. This is a GAP, not a
#     leaf-rooted defeater — the confound is an unestablished isolation, not positive
#     alternative-cause counter-evidence, so it lowers as an absent mandatory CQ, not an undercut.
#   * A second mandatory gap reinforces it: the null-effect half (ADVI/GSM at multiple batch
#     sizes) is unsupported by E01/E02 as written and rests on expected-outcome text; and no
#     variance CQ is supplied. Either mandatory gap suffices — no complete component_ablation
#     instance is declarable, so the claim lands E2-style gap.
#
# Expected verdict is at the bottom of this file (golden oracle).

artifact bam at sha256:b5d04d69e41d
policy corpus-v1
use backends [nd@1]

# --- The headline claim, stated but not assemblable at corpus-v1's evidential bar --------------

claim c06
  nl      = "BaM converges faster at larger batch sizes (dose levels up to B ~= D) in gradient evaluations, while ADVI and GSM show marginal or no improvement from larger batches"
  formal  = contributes(batch_size, bam, convergence_speed)
  binding = { author = m0-annotator, audit-status = reviewed }

# --- Evidence leaves are present ... ------------------------------------------------------------

# Principal premise: E01's BaM batch-size sweep (B=2 vs D-tuned) plus E04's B=10/100/300 cells,
# per result cell in the annotation and folded into refs.
leaf e1 : reports(e01, ablation_effect(batch_size, bam, convergence_speed, positive))
  kind       = observed
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [logic/experiments.md#E01, logic/experiments.md#E04, Figure-5-1, trace/exploration_tree.yaml#N11]

leaf e2 : matched_protocol(e01)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [logic/experiments.md#E01-Metrics, logic/experiments.md#E04-step-8]

# --- ... but no complete argument is declarable -------------------------------------------------

# (Intentionally no `arg`: isolated(batch_size, e01) has no honest leaf — the lambda_t = BD
# coupling confounds batch size with regularization strength except in E04 — and corpus-v1 makes
# single_variable mandatory. The null-effect half is likewise unsupported. Complete support is empty.)

status c06

# ==============================================================================================
